NOTES AND QUESTIONS ON SWIFT V. TYSON
1. Although Swift v. Tyson opines on the seemingly abstract question of what it means for something to be “law,” Swift’s discussion is firmly grounded in the text of a federal statute, the Rules of Decision Act, which at that time said:

The laws of the several States, except where the Constitution, treaties, or statutes of the United States otherwise require or provide, shall be regarded as rules of decision in trials at common law, in the courts of the United States, in cases where they apply.

